<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Genesee River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Genesee River rises in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> and flows north through western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> to Lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> at Rochester. Its valley was the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a>, westernmost nation of the Iroquois Confederacy, whose towns there were burned by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sullivan-expedition">Sullivan Expedition</a> in 1779. After the Revolution the fertile valley was sold to settlers, and the river's falls powered the mills of Rochester.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/genesee-river.jpg" alt="Genesee River" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pollinator at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Genesee_river_9044.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Haudenosaunee, the People of the Longhouse, were Iroquoian-speaking farming peoples of what is now upstate New York, living in palisaded villages of bark longhouses between the Hudson River and the <b>Genesee</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Seneca lived in western New York between Seneca Lake and the <b>Genesee River</b>, the westernmost of the five founding Haudenosaunee nations.</blockquote><a href="/state/seneca-nation">Seneca Nation</a></li></ul></section>
